<page>

<paragraphs>
    <title>In-Class Work</title>
    <p>
        These problems were completed during the in-class activity.
    </p>

<exercise workspace="0in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            The sum of a number and twenty-one times its reciprocal is <m>-10</m>. Find the number.
        </p>
    </statement>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            The speed of the current in Catamount Creek is 3 mph.  Sean can kayak 4 mi upstream in the same time that it takes him to kayak 10 mi downstream.  What is the speed of the kayak in still water?
        </p>
    </statement>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            A freight train covers 120 mi at its typical speed.  If the train travels 10 mph faster, the trip is 2 hr shorter.  How fast does the train typically travel?
        </p>
    </statement>

</exercise>

</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Hand-in Exercises</title>
    <p>
        The following problems will need to be written up carefully and turned in to the assignment on Canvas by the due date at the top of the first page.
    </p>

<exercise workspace="0in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            The sum of the reciprocal of a number and the reciprocal of the number two greater is 5/12. Find (both possibilities for) the two numbers.
        </p>
    </statement>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Annette’s paddleboat travels 3 km/h in still water.  The boat is paddled 10 km downstream in the same time that it takes to go 2 km upstream.  What is the speed of the river? 
        </p>
    </statement>

</exercise>

</paragraphs>
</page>
